Lets the app's UI share Chromium's Wayland display on Linux (decision 3), as a subsurface of the
browser window: chrome exports its libwayland's client API, so the app's calls reach the copy the
display came from; Chromium tags its surfaces, so pointer events on the UI's aren't taken for its
own, and a drag over them keeps Chromium's offer of it, as wlroots ends a drag whose offer goes;
the core can reach a window's surface through the WaylandProxy, made outside tests too, and ask
it whether the compositor's answer to the state a window has is latched; and a hook runs the
core after each dispatch of Chromium's events, for the UI's that its reads queued.
Hooks only: the logic is in browser_core/platform/linux/wayland.cc.
